This quickstart explains how to use ShroudForge with an Enshrouded Dedicated Server. Your PC and the server are separate installations. Installing a mod on one does not install it on the other.

== Choose a server setup ==

ShroudForge currently has guides for these four server setups:

* [[Multiplayer: Windows Dedicated Server|Windows Dedicated Server]] covers the Enshrouded server installed through Steam or managed by a Windows hosting panel.
* [[Multiplayer: Mornedhel Docker server (Proton)|Mornedhel Docker server with Proton]] covers the Proton image and its required ShroudForge DLL override.
* [[Multiplayer: Mornedhel Docker server (Wine)|Mornedhel Docker server with Wine]] covers the Wine image tags. Read its compatibility note because the current ShroudForge Docker instructions specifically document the Proton override.
* [[Multiplayer: Install ShroudForge on GPortal|GPortal Dedicated Server]] covers the community-reported file-upload workaround and its GPortal-specific restrictions.

Windows and Mornedhel Docker use the shared file setup in [[Multiplayer: Install server files|Install ShroudForge server files]]. GPortal has a separate upload process because its file manager rejects the two ShroudForge executable files. Follow the [[Multiplayer: Install ShroudForge on GPortal|GPortal guide]] for that setup.

== Install a mod on the server ==

Stop the server and back up its world before changing its files. Copy the complete ShroudForge release beside <code>enshrouded_server.exe</code>, then copy the mod package into the server's <code>mods</code> folder. Follow [[Multiplayer: Install server files|the shared file installation steps]].

Client and server installations are independent. Install a mod on your PC, the Dedicated Server, or both according to the mod's process targets and its installation instructions. A client-only install does not make the mod run on a Dedicated Server.

== Start and check the server ==

Start the Dedicated Server using its normal Steam, Docker, or hosting-panel method. Check <code>shroudforge/logs/shroudforge-server.log</code> in the server installation or its mounted host folder. The log confirms whether ShroudForge started and reports mod loading errors.

Mods that change game files need their preparation step before the server starts. Follow the mod's article for its required target and restart behavior.

